# GhostWire Web3 Decentralization Technology Roadmap

> **Scope correction (2026-10):** Web3 here means **decentralization technology** —
> architectural patterns, protocols, and mechanisms that make systems resilient,
> censorship-resistant, and decentralized. Token economics, DeFi yields, trading,
> NFTs, money flows, incentive tokens, and payment settlement are explicitly out
> of scope. GhostWire is an anonymous mesh network; the value of Web3 to it is in
> *how* decentralized systems are built.

GhostWire already ships several of these primitives in `ghostwire-libs/crates/`
(`ghostwire-dtn`, `hlc-rs`, `sphinx-rs`, `trust-store`). This document maps the
remaining decentralization techniques to concrete GhostWire applications.

---

## 1. Decentralized Identity (DIDs, VCs, SSI)

**Core mechanism.** A Decentralized Identifier (DID) is a globally unique
identifier (`did:<method>:<id>`) resolved to a DID Document containing public
keys, authentication methods, and service endpoints — with no central registry.
Verifiable Credentials (VCs) are cryptographically signed attestations issued by
one party, held by a subject, and verified by any third party against the
issuer's public key. Self-Sovereign Identity (SSI) puts key control in the
owner's hands; the verifier needs no central authority, only the issuer's public
key.

**Examples.** W3C DID/VC specs; Sovrin/Hyperledger Indy adapted to IoT mesh
(Schnorr signatures, simplex/broadcast connections); libp2p peer IDs as implicit
DIDs.

**GhostWire lesson.** Replace OIDC-style central auth with **local Ed25519 +
X25519 keypairs as node DIDs** (already planned). Node handle → pubkey mapping
lives in the Kademlia DHT (§4), not a server. VCs can attest roles ("verified
rescue team", "trusted relay") so peers grant priority without a CA. Keep DIDs
offline-resolvable: cache DID documents in the mesh so identity works with no
internet.

## 2. Content-Addressed Storage (IPFS, Merkle DAGs)

**Core mechanism.** Content is identified by a cryptographic hash of its bytes
(CID), not by location. A Merkle DAG links immutable blocks via hashes; any
tampering changes the root hash, so integrity is verifiable by anyone holding
the hash. Data is replicated across peers; availability degrades gracefully.

**Examples.** IPFS (CID + BitSwap block exchange + Merkle DAG), Git object
model.

**GhostWire lesson.** Address mesh messages, files, and channel state by
**content hash (CID-style)** rather than sender-assigned IDs. This gives
tamper-evident messages, automatic deduplication, and the ability to fetch a
block from any peer that has it — ideal for fragmented mesh segments.
Store-and-forward (§6) becomes "replicate blocks by hash."

## 3. Consensus Without Central Coordination (CRDTs, Gossip, Vector Clocks)

**Core mechanism.** CRDTs are data structures whose merge function is a bounded
join-semilattice (commutative, associative, idempotent), so concurrent updates
converge deterministically with no coordinator. Vector clocks track causality
(`(node, counter)` pairs) and detect conflicts; hybrid logical clocks (HLC)
combine physical time + logical counters for total order without synced clocks.
Gossip epidemically disseminates state in O(k log n) messages.

**Examples.** Amazon Dynamo (vector clocks), Riak (CRDTs), Cassandra gossip,
`hlc-rs` (already in GhostWire).

**GhostWire lesson.** Model mutable shared state (channel membership, presence,
routing tables) as **CRDTs merged via gossip**, ordered by the existing
`hlc-rs`. This eliminates central coordination for state agreement and tolerates
partitions. Keep conflict resolution at the data-structure level (commutative
ops), not application-level last-write-wins, to avoid silent data loss.

## 4. Routing Resilience (DHTs, MANET Routing, Epidemic Routing)

**Core mechanism.** Kademlia uses XOR-distance metric + k-buckets + parallel
(α=3) asynchronous lookups, giving O(log n) hops and fast failure tolerance.
Chord (ring + finger tables) and Pastry (prefix routing) are alternatives;
S-Kademlia adds trust/reputation against Sybil. For wireless mesh, BATMAN/OLSR
provide link-state routing; epidemic/Spray-and-Wait routing handles intermittent
connectivity.

**Examples.** Kademlia (BitTorrent, IPFS, Ethereum), BATMAN-adv, OLSR,
Spray-and-Wait, PRoPHET (already in `ghostwire-dtn`).

**GhostWire lesson.** Implement **Kademlia DHT for handle/peer lookup** (already
in engineering goals) with S-Kademlia-style reputation to resist Sybil in a
permissionless mesh. Combine with epidemic Spray-and-Wait + PRoPHET (already
shipped) for DTN segments. Use parallel lookups so a single dead node doesn't
block resolution.

## 5. Privacy-Preserving Communication (Onion Routing, Mixnets, Noise, PFS)

**Core mechanism.** Onion routing wraps messages in layered encryption; each hop
peels one layer, knowing only predecessor/successor. Mixnets add shuffling +
delays for stronger metadata (unlinkability) protection at latency cost. The
Noise protocol framework provides authenticated key exchange with forward
secrecy; perfect forward secrecy (PFS) ensures past sessions stay secret even if
keys leak later. Cover traffic (dummy messages) hides traffic patterns.

**Examples.** Tor (onion routing), Sphinx (already in `sphinx-rs`), Noise
(libp2p, WireGuard), Nym (mixnet).

**GhostWire lesson.** GhostWire already has `sphinx-rs` (bitwise-unlinkable
onion routing with SURBs) — **make it the default transport for sensitive
traffic**, not an option. Add Noise-based handshakes with PFS for all peer
links, and optional cover traffic on high-risk links. Keep onion routing's
latency tradeoff explicit: onion for anonymity-critical, direct Noise for
latency-critical.

## 6. Offline-First Protocols (DTN, Bundle Protocol, Store-and-Forward)

**Core mechanism.** DTN adds a bundle layer (RFC 9171) over transports:
messages are self-contained bundles with source/dest EIDs, lifetime, and
custody transfer. Intermediate nodes store bundles until a contact opportunity,
then forward; custody transfer shifts delivery responsibility. Convergence Layer
Adapters (CLAs) map bundles onto any underlying link.

**Examples.** NASA DTN, Bundle Protocol v7, Reticulum (already working in
GhostWire).

**GhostWire lesson.** GhostWire's Reticulum transport + `ghostwire-dtn` already
implement this. Formalize **bundle lifetimes + custody transfer** as
first-class in the transport manager so messages survive partitions with
explicit expiry and accountability. Add CLA abstraction so the same bundle layer
runs over BLE, WiFi Direct, LoRa, etc.

## 7. Key Management and Trust (Threshold Sigs, MPC, Social Recovery)

**Core mechanism.** Threshold signatures (t-of-n) require a subset to sign; the
private key is never materialized. MPC splits keys into shares across nodes;
DKG generates shares without a trusted dealer. Social recovery splits a root
secret via Shamir into shares held by trusted contacts. TEEs protect shares in
hardware.

**Examples.** MuSig/Schnorr threshold, DFNS MPC, Sui multisig, Shamir secret
sharing.

**GhostWire lesson.** For high-value node identities (rescue team keys, relay
authority), use **threshold signatures (t-of-n) across multiple devices** so no
single compromised device can impersonate. For ordinary users, keep simple
single-key Ed25519 but add **social recovery** (Shamir shares to trusted
contacts) to avoid lockout. Never store full keys in software; prefer OS
keystore/TEE.

## 8. Self-Healing and Fault Tolerance (Gossip Failure Detection)

**Core mechanism.** SWIM replaces O(n²) heartbeating with: ping one random
peer per round; on timeout, ask k peers to indirectly probe; mark suspect →
(after timeout without refutation) failed. Suspicion piggybacks on gossip.
Phi-accrual outputs a continuous suspicion score adapting to latency variance.
Lifeguard adds self-reporting degraded nodes.

**Examples.** HashiCorp memberlist (SWIM), Cassandra (phi-accrual + gossip),
Consul.

**GhostWire lesson.** Add **SWIM-style membership + failure detection** to the
mesh layer: O(1) per-node load, indirect probing to avoid false positives on
asymmetric partitions (common in wireless), suspicion piggybacked on existing
gossip. Use phi-accrual thresholds tuned per link type (BLE vs WiFi). This
directly improves the "2/12 transports working" reliability story.

## 9. Decentralized Naming and Discovery (ENS, mDNS, DNS-SD, Rendezvous)

**Core mechanism.** mDNS/DNS-SD uses IP multicast DNS records for zero-config
local discovery (`_p2p._udp.local`). libp2p rendezvous lets peers exchange
multiaddresses securely via a rendezvous point. DHT-based naming (ENS-style)
maps human names to keys without a central DNS.

**Examples.** libp2p mDNS, DNS-SD, libp2p rendezvous, ENS.

**GhostWire lesson.** Use **mDNS/DNS-SD for zero-config LAN discovery** (already
natural for libp2p), **libp2p rendezvous for cross-network peer introduction**,
and the **Kademlia DHT for human-readable handles → pubkey**. No DNS, no
registrar, works offline on local links.

## 10. Zero-Knowledge Proofs and Selective Disclosure

**Core mechanism.** ZK proofs let a prover convince a verifier a statement is
true without revealing the underlying data. Selective disclosure (ZK over VCs)
proves predicates (age>18, role=rescue) without revealing identity attributes.
Client-side proving keeps raw data on device.

**Examples.** zk-SNARKs (Zcash, Aztec), W3C VC + ZK extension, Midnight's
programmable privacy.

**GhostWire lesson.** For anonymous mesh, ZK allows **proving membership/role
without revealing identity** — e.g., prove "I am a verified rescue responder"
without exposing which responder. Apply to priority queuing (QoS tiers) so
emergency traffic gets precedence without deanonymizing the sender. Keep proofs
client-side; never transmit raw attributes.

