## 11. Autonomous Research: Unrelated Technologies Applicable to GhostWire

### 11.1 Mesh Routing: Babel (RFC 8966) over BATMAN/OLSR
**Finding:** Babel (RFC 8966) outperformed both OLSR and BATMAN in real UAV
mesh tests — it is mobility-designed, link-agnostic, and IETF-standardized.
BATMAN-adv has slow convergence (~10s for link quality updates) and poor
mobility. OLSR is good for dense networks but its MPR flooding is suboptimal
for sparse topologies.

**GhostWire lesson:** For IP-layer mesh routing over WiFi/BLE links, consider
**Babel** as the primary routing protocol with OLSRv2 as fallback for dense
deployments. Babel runs over any link (IBSS, 802.11s, wired), decoupling
routing from chipset mesh-mode quirks. This is directly applicable to GhostWire's
WiFi Direct and ManagedWiFi transports.

### 11.2 Offline-First State Sync: Automerge CRDTs
**Finding:** Automerge 2.0 rewrote its core in Rust (compiled to WASM), cutting
memory usage ~10x. It provides CRDT-backed JSON documents with efficient sync
(bloom filter → delta exchange). Yjs uses YATA algorithm for text/sequences.
Electric SQL brings CRDT-style sync to PostgreSQL.

**GhostWire lesson:** Use Automerge's Rust core for **offline-first channel state,
presence, and routing table sync** across mesh nodes. Automerge works over any
transport (including one-way channels), making it ideal for DTN segments where
connectivity is intermittent. This complements the existing CRDT+HLC plan (§3).

### 11.3 DTN: IBR-DTN
**Finding:** IBR-DTN is a reference DTN implementation with bundle protocol v7
support, convergence layer adapters, and custody transfer. It has been deployed
in challenged networks (rural, disaster, space).

**GhostWire lesson:** GhostWire already has Reticulum (which implements DTN
concepts). Formalize the **CLA (Convergence Layer Adapter) abstraction** so the
same bundle layer runs over BLE, WiFi Direct, LoRa, etc. — exactly as IBR-DTN
does. This is already in the plan (§6).

### 11.4 P2P Discovery: libp2p Rendezvous
**Finding:** libp2p rendezvous protocol allows peers to register and discover
each other through a rendezvous point, without a central server. It supports
namespace-based discovery and authenticated registration.

**GhostWire lesson:** Use **libp2p rendezvous** (already available in the
libp2p crate) for cross-network peer introduction when mDNS is unavailable
(internet-connected but no LAN). This complements mDNS (LAN) and Kademlia DHT
(global).

### 11.5 Privacy: Nym Mixnet
**Finding:** Nym is a mixnet (not onion routing) that provides stronger
metadata protection than Tor by adding timing obfuscation and cover traffic.
It has higher latency (~100-500ms) but is more resistant to traffic analysis.

**GhostWire lesson:** For **ultra-sensitive traffic** (whistleblower, journalist),
consider Nym-style mixnet as an additional anonymity layer beyond Sphinx.
The latency tradeoff is acceptable for non-real-time communication. Keep Sphinx
for real-time, Nym for store-and-forward.

### 11.6 Local-First Databases: SQLite + CRDT
**Finding:** SQLite with CRDT extensions (e.g., cr-sqlite) enables local-first
databases that sync automatically. Electric SQL syncs Postgres to local SQLite.

**GhostWire lesson:** Use **cr-sqlite** for local-first persistence of mesh
state (routing tables, channel membership, presence). Changes sync automatically
via CRDT merge when peers reconnect. This is simpler than building custom sync
and leverages SQLite's reliability.

### Prioritized Addition to Top 5
6. **Babel routing for WiFi/BLE mesh links** (§11.1) — mobility-designed,
   standardized, outperforms BATMAN in real tests.
7. **Automerge Rust core for offline state sync** (§11.2) — 10x memory
   improvement, works over any transport including DTN.
8. **cr-sqlite for local-first persistence** (§11.6) — automatic CRDT sync
   via SQLite, simpler than custom sync.
